!deck: Math::Calculus
!tags: Math Calculus Differentiation Derivative-Test
!type: Cloze Math

!id: zzKitpg6bx
!text: A {{c3::critical point of \( f \)}} is an {{c1::interior point \( c \) of the domain of \( f \)}} at which {{c2::either \( f'(c) = 0 \) or \( f'(c) \) fails to exist::condition}}.
~~~

!id: GNUskJIVPy
!text: The main idea of the derivative test is to classify a {{c2::critical point \( c \)}} as a {{c1::local maximum}}, {{c1::local minimum}}, or {{c1::inconclusive}} by comparing the {{c2::sign of \( f' \) on the two halves}} of {{c2::a punctured neighborhood of \( c \)}}.
~~~

!id: ZBp09xCVc5
!text: At a critical point \( c \), the first derivative test requires \( f \) to be {{c1::continuous at \( c \)}} and {{c2::differentiable on a punctured neighborhood of \( c \)}}. The derivative \( f'(c) \) itself may be {{c3::zero or undefined}}.
~~~

!id: 6AllLxbkmN
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f' \) changes from positive to negative across \( c \), then \( f \) has a {{c1::strict local maximum}} at \( c \).
~~~

!id: eOBg5g4d3w
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f' \) changes from negative to positive across \( c \), then \( f \) has a {{c1::strict local minimum}} at \( c \).
~~~

!id: hivA9FTtp5
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f'>0 \) throughout a punctured neighborhood of \( c \), then \( f \) is {{c1::strictly increasing through \( c \)}} and \( c \) is {{c1::not a local extremum}}.
~~~

!id: TqaJZVuBH3
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f'<0 \) throughout a punctured neighborhood of \( c \), then \( f \) is {{c1::strictly decreasing through \( c \)}} and \( c \) is {{c1::not a local extremum}}.
~~~

!id: Kl0wfuwlv0
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \), \( f'>0 \) just left of \( c \) makes \( f \) {{c1::increase toward \( c \)}}, while \( f'<0 \) just right makes it {{c1::decrease away from \( c \)}}. Thus \( f(x)<f(c) \) nearby, giving a {{c1::strict local maximum}}.
~~~

!id: 0oiaZNkT0Y
!text: If \( f \) is continuous at \( c \), \( f'<0 \) just left of \( c \) makes \( f \) {{c1::decrease toward \( c \)}}, while \( f'>0 \) just right makes it {{c1::increase away from \( c \)}}. Thus \( f(x)>f(c) \) nearby, giving a {{c1::strict local minimum}}.
~~~

!id: 5PCIVmIycT
!text: At \( c=0 \), {{c1::\( f(x)=x^3 \)}} has \( f'(0)=0 \) but \( f'(x)=3x^2>0 \) for every \( x\ne0 \). Thus \( 0 \) is a stationary critical point that is not a local extremum.
~~~

!id: 1SZHDBSD9i
!text: Under the first derivative test hypotheses, a change of \( f' \) from positive to negative across \( c \) is {{c1::sufficient}} for a strict local maximum at \( c \).
~~~

!id: KG1TfTWiGg
!text: A fixed sign change of \( f' \) across a critical point is {{c1::not necessary}} for a local extremum: \( f' \) may {{c1::oscillate in sign arbitrarily close to the point}} while an extremum still exists. The first derivative test is then inconclusive.
!related: For \( f(x) = x^2(2 + \sin(1/x)) \), \( f(0) = 0 \) is a local (indeed global) minimum, yet \( f' \) oscillates in sign arbitrarily close to \( 0 \).
~~~

!id: iZBE6xycSb
!text: The second derivative test uses {{c1::the sign of \( f''(c) \)}} to classify a {{c2::stationary critical point \( c \), where \( f'(c)=0 \)}} as a local maximum, a local minimum, or inconclusive.
~~~

!id: hQVhdbKFL8
!text: At \( c \), the second derivative test requires {{c1::\( f'(c)=0 \)}} and {{c2::a finite second derivative \( f''(c) \)}}. Continuity of \( f'' \) on a neighborhood is not required.
~~~

!id: KuycefiBQ8
!text: In the second derivative test, \( f'(c)=0 \) and \( f''(c)>0 \) imply a {{c1::strict local minimum}} at \( c \).
!back extra: ![Second derivative test: f''(c) > 0 gives a local minimum, f''(c) < 0 gives a local maximum](img/second-derivative-test.svg)
~~~

!id: lK3nBoG7DS
!text: In the second derivative test, \( f'(c)=0 \) and \( f''(c)<0 \) imply a {{c1::strict local maximum}} at \( c \).
!back extra: ![Second derivative test: f''(c) > 0 gives a local minimum, f''(c) < 0 gives a local maximum](img/second-derivative-test.svg)
~~~

!id: gndbllwJqo
!text: If \( f'(c)=0 \) and \( f''(c)=0 \), the second derivative test is {{c1::inconclusive}}: \( c \) may be a local minimum, a local maximum, or neither.
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: v6Xvb6755z
!text: If \( f'' \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f''(c)>0 \), then \( f'' \) is {{c1::positive on some neighborhood of \( c \)}}. Hence \( f' \) is {{c1::strictly increasing there}}.
~~~

!id: KKNFd5iBoW
!text: If \( f'' \) is continuous at \( c \) and \( f''(c)<0 \), then \( f'' \) is {{c1::negative on some neighborhood of \( c \)}}. Hence \( f' \) is {{c1::strictly decreasing there}}.
~~~

!id: pCV9LoXhmk
!text: If \( f'(c)=0 \) and \( f''(c)>0 \), the limit defining \( f''(c) \) makes \( f' \) {{c1::negative just left of \( c \) and positive just right of \( c \)}}. The first derivative test then gives a strict local minimum; no neighborhood-wide monotonicity of \( f' \) is needed.
!back extra: ![Second derivative test: f''(c) > 0 gives a local minimum, f''(c) < 0 gives a local maximum](img/second-derivative-test.svg)
~~~

!id: bQYfeBqPRF
!text: If \( f'(c)=0 \) and \( f''(c)<0 \), the limit defining \( f''(c) \) makes \( f' \) {{c1::positive just left of \( c \) and negative just right of \( c \)}}. The first derivative test then gives a strict local maximum; no neighborhood-wide monotonicity of \( f' \) is needed.
!back extra: ![Second derivative test: f''(c) > 0 gives a local minimum, f''(c) < 0 gives a local maximum](img/second-derivative-test.svg)
~~~

!id: PE69rMlBNy
!text: At \( c=0 \), {{c1::\( f(x)=x^4 \)}} has \( f'(0)=f''(0)=0 \) yet a local minimum, since \( f(x)\ge f(0) \) nearby.
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: 5KjIeNMbeS
!text: At \( c=0 \), {{c1::\( f(x)=-x^4 \)}} has \( f'(0)=f''(0)=0 \) yet a local maximum, since \( f(x)\le f(0) \) nearby.
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: Iw4CpH3lwc
!text: At \( c=0 \), {{c1::\( f(x)=x^3 \)}} has \( f'(0)=f''(0)=0 \) but no local extremum, since it is strictly increasing through \( 0 \).
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: kYHL8gErG0
!text: The first derivative test uses {{c1::the signs of \( f' \) on both sides of \( c \)}}, while the second derivative test uses {{c1::the single value \( f''(c) \)}}. Thus the first may classify \( c \) even when \( f''(c)=0 \) or does not exist.
~~~

!id: hpI2gfu2jd
!text: Derivative tests describe behavior {{c1::near a critical point}}, so by themselves they classify only local extrema. Absolute extrema require comparing values {{c1::across the entire domain or interval, including endpoints when relevant}}.
~~~

!id: 0dc9nxVRUY
!text: On an interval where \( f \) is differentiable and concave up, its graph lies {{c1::on or above each of its tangent lines}}.
!back extra: ![Concave down vs. concave up: the graph relative to its tangent lines](img/concavity.svg)
~~~

!id: TwPfydUnYy
!text: On an interval where \( f \) is differentiable and concave down, its graph lies {{c1::on or below each of its tangent lines}}.
!back extra: ![Concave down vs. concave up: the graph relative to its tangent lines](img/concavity.svg)
~~~

!id: BdQFccaRgN
!text: For differentiable \( f \) on an interval \( I \), \( f \) is concave up exactly when \( f' \) is {{c1::nondecreasing}} on \( I \), and concave down exactly when \( f' \) is {{c1::nonincreasing}} on \( I \).
!back extra: ![Concave down vs. concave up: the graph relative to its tangent lines](img/concavity.svg)
~~~

!id: 1PWJif61Og
!text: If \( f''(x)>0 \) throughout an interval \( I \), then \( f' \) is {{c1::strictly increasing}} and \( f \) is {{c1::concave up}} on \( I \).
!back extra: ![Concave down vs. concave up: the graph relative to its tangent lines](img/concavity.svg)
~~~

!id: Z31QV1NBIZ
!text: If \( f''(x)<0 \) throughout an interval \( I \), then \( f' \) is {{c1::strictly decreasing}} and \( f \) is {{c1::concave down}} on \( I \).
!back extra: ![Concave down vs. concave up: the graph relative to its tangent lines](img/concavity.svg)
~~~

!id: Ef6m6NkEhu
!text: An {{c3::inflection point of \( f \)}} is a point \( c \) where \( f \) is {{c1::continuous}} and its concavity {{c2::changes across \( c \), from up on one side to down on the other or vice versa}}.
!back extra: ![Inflection points of f(x) = sin(x) at x = k*pi: concavity alternates across each, with a diagonal (non-horizontal) tangent line at every crossing](img/inflection-point.svg)
~~~

!id: 76QRIe0yYc
!text: If \( f''(c) \) exists and \( c \) is an inflection point, then {{c1::\( f''(c)=0 \)}}: the reversal of concavity makes \( f' \) have {{c1::a local extremum}} at \( c \).
!back extra: ![Inflection points of f(x) = sin(x) at x = k*pi, with f'(x) = cos(x) overlaid: f' has a local extremum exactly at each c_i, confirming f''(c) = 0](img/inflection-point.svg)
~~~

!id: bNYWru4qZW
!text: Provided \( f''(c) \) exists, \( f''(c)=0 \) is {{c1::necessary}} for \( c \) to be an inflection point: concavity reversal gives \( f' \) a local extremum, whose derivative at \( c \) must vanish.
!back extra: ![Inflection points of f(x) = sin(x) at x = k*pi, with f'(x) = cos(x) overlaid: f' has a local extremum exactly at each c_i, confirming f''(c) = 0](img/inflection-point.svg)
~~~

!id: Vi6Lt67A78
!text: The condition \( f''(c)=0 \) is {{c1::not sufficient}} for an inflection point: the concavity may {{c1::stay the same on both sides of \( c \)}}.
!related: For \( f(x) = x^4 \) at \( c = 0 \), \( f''(0) = 0 \) but \( f \) is concave up on all of \( \mathbb{R} \), so \( 0 \) is not an inflection point.
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: 3v9WbaSYKt
!text: A counterexample to “\( f''(c)=0 \) implies an inflection point” is {{c1::\( f(x)=x^4 \) at \( c=0 \)}}: \( f''(x)=12x^2 \) vanishes at \( 0 \) but never changes sign, so concavity does not reverse.
!related: The same function already shows \( f''(c) = 0 \) does not determine whether \( c \) is a local extremum.
!back extra: ![f''(c) = 0 is inconclusive: x^4 (min), -x^4 (max), and x^3 (not an extremum) at c = 0](img/dtest-inconclusive.svg)
~~~

!id: 7t0LUb3vlw
!text: The function \( f(x)=x^2 \) has {{c1::no inflection points}} because \( f''(x)={{c1::2}}>0 \) everywhere, so concavity never reverses.
~~~

!id: 93PmhfKWxk
!text: The function \( f(x)=x^3 \) has an inflection point at {{c1::\( x=0 \)}}: \( f''(x)={{c1::6x}} \) is negative to the left and positive to the right, so concavity changes from down to up.
~~~

!id: KtwnhA3Nu0
!text: The function \( f(x)=\sin(x) \) has inflection points at {{c1::every \( x=k\pi \), \( k\in\mathbb{Z} \)}} because \( f''(x)={{c1::-\sin(x)}} \) changes sign at each of those points.
!back extra: ![Inflection points of f(x) = sin(x) at x = k*pi: concavity alternates across each, with a diagonal (non-horizontal) tangent line at every crossing](img/inflection-point.svg)
~~~

!id: XCKBd5w3hb
!text: At a stationary point \( c \), \( f''(c)>0 \) gives a {{c1::strict local minimum}} and \( f''(c)<0 \) gives a {{c1::strict local maximum}}. The sign of \( f'' \) at this one point {{c3::does not by itself establish concavity on a whole neighborhood}}.
!back extra: ![Second derivative test: f''(c) > 0 gives a local minimum, f''(c) < 0 gives a local maximum](img/second-derivative-test.svg)
~~~
